{{- /*
  support/message-comment-key — the idempotency rule for logging Slack
  thread messages as bead comments.

  Every prompt that mirrors thread messages onto a bead (watch-channel,
  housekeeping, check-status, investigate, continue-conversation) must
  dedup on the Slack message `ts`, never on the comment text: re-rendered
  headers (different clock time, `@name` vs `<@Uxxxx>`, trimmed greeting)
  made text-matching fail and the same customer message was appended once
  per cycle.

  Whitespace contract identical to the other support fragments: fragment
  body is column-1 Markdown with NO leading and NO trailing newline;
  callers use plain `{{ template ... }}` and rely on the surrounding YAML
  newlines for spacing.
*/ -}}
🔑 **Dedup thread messages by Slack `ts`, never by text.** Every Slack message has a stable
`ts` (e.g. `1753693512.874319`). Put it in the comment header as the **last** field:

```
**[TAG @author · YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM UTC · ts:<slack_ts>]**

<content in markdown>
```

Before writing **any** message comment: read `bd comments <id>` once, collect every `ts:<...>`
already present, and **skip** the message if its `ts` is in that set. A message is "already
captured" **iff** its `ts` appears — do not judge by how similar the text looks, and do not
re-add a message just because the header you would render now differs from the stored one
(different clock time, `@name` vs `<@Uxxxx>`, trimmed greeting). Conversely, only messages
with a `ts` you have never stored are new.

If a tool genuinely does not return a `ts` for a message, use `ts:unknown-<YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM>`
(the message's own Slack timestamp, not the current time) so the key is still stable across runs.

`ts:` keys apply **only** to comments that mirror a Slack message (`[INBOUND]` / `[CONTEXT]` /
`[OUTBOUND]`). Bookkeeping comments (`[STATE]`, `[PRIORITY]`, `[CONTEXT investigation]`, drafts)
have no `ts` and are exempt.
{{- /* no trailing newline */ -}}
